				<h1>Watermark (1988)</h1>
				
				<a class="new-amazon-album-link" href="https://amzn.to/49c6ZNC" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">
					<Image  src={wm} alt="Watermark (1988)"  width="300" height="300" /></a>

					<h2>Commercial Breakthrough and Release</h2>
				
				<p><strong><em>Watermark</em></strong> was released on <strong>19 September 1988</strong> by <strong>WEA Records</strong> as the landmark second studio album by Irish singer and composer <strong>Enya</strong>.</p>

<p>The album marked her global commercial breakthrough, reaching <strong>number 5</strong> on the <strong>UK Albums Chart</strong> and <strong>number 25</strong> on the US <strong>Billboard 200</strong> while selling over eight million copies worldwide.</p>

<h3>Genre and Recording Process</h3>

<p>Although widely regarded as a seminal milestone in <a href="/new-age-music/">New Age music</a>, <strong>Enya</strong> does not classify her compositions within that specific genre.</p>

<p>Recorded between June 1987 and April 1988, initial demo production began at <strong>Aigle Studio</strong> in Dublin before relocating to <strong>Orinoco Studios</strong> in London for digital re-recording.</p>

<p>Executive producer <strong>Rob Dickins</strong> signed <strong>Enya</strong> to <strong>WEA</strong> after falling in love with her 1987 self-titled debut, granting her total artistic freedom without strict deadlines.</p>

<p><a href="https://amzn.to/49c6ZNC" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Stream or download Enya songs</a></p>

<h3>Album Themes and Central Imagery</h3>

<blockquote>
    <p>
        Looking back on Watermark, the words are those of loss, of reflection, of exile — not necessarily from one's country, but from those whom the heart loves. It has in its theme searching, longing, of reaching out for an answer. The ocean is a central image. It is the symbolism of a great journey, which is the way I would describe this album.
    </p>
    <em>Enya</em>
</blockquote>

<h2>Album Editions and Special Tracks</h2>

<p>The original European release from <strong>WEA Records</strong> is eleven tracks long with a total running time of <strong>40:05</strong>.</p>

<p>To expand international releases, <strong>Reprise Records</strong> and <strong>Geffen Records</strong> added a twelfth track to North American pressings, though some reissues omit it.</p>

<h3>Track Version Highlights</h3>

<ul>
    <li><strong>"Storms in Africa II"</strong>: A faster-tempo remix of <a href="#Storms_In_Africa">"Storms in Africa"</a> featuring English lyrics replacing the original Irish Gaelic.</li>
    <li><strong>"Orinoco Flow"</strong>: The final song composed for the album, built around customized pizzicato chords generated on a <strong>Roland D-50</strong> synthesizer.</li>
    <li><strong>2009 Japanese Reissue</strong>: Expanded the album to thirteen tracks by adding the bonus track <strong>"Morning Glory"</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Singles and Film Soundtracks</h2>

<p><strong>WEA Records</strong> issued four official singles between <strong>1988</strong> and <strong>1991</strong> to support the record's worldwide promotion.</p>

<p>The track <a href="#Exile">"Exile"</a> gained widespread cinematic exposure after being featured on the soundtracks of the films <em>Green Card</em> (1990) and <em>L.A. Story</em> (1991).</p>

<h3>Official Singles Timeline</h3>

<ul>
    <li><a href="#Orinoco_Flow">"Orinoco Flow"</a> (<strong>3 October 1988</strong>): Surged to <strong>number 1</strong> on the <strong>UK Singles Chart</strong> for three weeks and received two <strong>Grammy Award</strong> nominations.</li>
    <li><a href="#Evening_Falls...">"Evening Falls..."</a> (<strong>12 December 1988</strong>): Reached <strong>number 3</strong> in Ireland and <strong>number 20</strong> on the UK Singles Chart.</li>
    <li><a href="#Storms_In_Africa">"Storms In Africa"</a> (<strong>5 June 1989</strong>): Top 15 hit in Ireland featuring African hand drums and rototoms by guest percussionist <strong>Chris Hughes</strong>.</li>
    <li><a href="#Exile">"Exile"</a> (<strong>20 May 1991</strong>): Released as the fourth single, highlighted by a low whistle performance by <strong>Davy Spillane</strong>.</li>
</ul>

